@{
    ViewData["Title"] = "Monitoring & Logging in Dockerized Microservices";
    ViewData["ActiveMenu"] = "Docker";
    ViewData["ActivePage"] = "MonitoringMicroservices";
}

<div class="container py-4">
    <p class="text-center lead">
        Observability is key in microservices. Docker makes it possible to integrate powerful monitoring and logging tools like ELK, Prometheus, and Grafana to gain insights into your services.
    </p>
    <hr />

    <!-- Section: ELK Stack Logging -->
    <div class="mt-5">
        <h2>Logging with the ELK Stack</h2>
        <p>
            ELK stands for Elasticsearch, Logstash, and Kibana. It's widely used to collect, index, and visualize logs from multiple services.
        </p>

        <p>Logstash reads logs, Elasticsearch stores them, and Kibana visualizes them:</p>

        <div class="code-snippet bg-light p-3 rounded position-relative">
            <pre class="mb-0"><code>docker-compose up -d elasticsearch logstash kibana</code></pre>
            <button class="btn btn-outline-secondary copy-btn position-absolute top-0 end-0 m-2">Copy</button>
        </div>

        <p>Then configure your microservices to output logs in JSON format for parsing.</p>
    </div>

    <!-- Section: Prometheus & Grafana Monitoring -->
    <div class="mt-5">
        <h3>Monitoring with Prometheus & Grafana</h3>
        <p>
            Prometheus scrapes metrics from microservices and Grafana displays them through dashboards.
        </p>

        <p>Sample Prometheus config:</p>
        <div class="code-snippet bg-light p-3 rounded position-relative">
            <pre class="mb-0"><code>scrape_configs:
  - job_name: 'user-service'
    static_configs:
      - targets: ['user-service:9100']</code></pre>
            <button class="btn btn-outline-secondary copy-btn position-absolute top-0 end-0 m-2">Copy</button>
        </div>

        <p>Microservices should expose metrics on endpoints like <code>/metrics</code>.</p>
    </div>

    <!-- Section: Debugging Containers -->
    <div class="mt-5">
        <h3>Debugging Running Containers</h3>
        <p>Use these commands to inspect or attach to running containers:</p>

        <div class="code-snippet bg-light p-3 rounded position-relative">
            <pre class="mb-0"><code>docker logs user-service
docker exec -it user-service /bin/sh</code></pre>
            <button class="btn btn-outline-secondary copy-btn position-absolute top-0 end-0 m-2">Copy</button>
        </div>

        <p>Useful for live debugging, inspecting environment, or troubleshooting failures.</p>
    </div>

    <!-- Section: Visualizing Inter-Service Calls -->
    <div class="mt-5">
        <h3>Visualizing API Calls Between Services</h3>
        <p>
            Use tools like Grafana Tempo, Jaeger, or Zipkin to track distributed traces across microservices.
            These tools show the full path of a request as it travels through services.
        </p>
    </div>

    <!-- Summary -->
    <div class="mt-5">
        <p>
            With centralized logging and real-time monitoring, you can proactively detect issues, understand system health,
            and debug microservices efficiently. Let’s now compare Docker with Kubernetes for advanced orchestration.
        </p>
    </div>

    <!-- Navigation Buttons -->
    <div class="d-flex justify-content-between mt-4">
        <a href="@Url.Action("SecuringMicroservices", "Docker")" class="btn btn-outline-secondary">
            &larr; Previous: Securing Dockerized Microservices
        </a>
        <a href="@Url.Action("DockerVsKubernetesMicroservices", "Docker")" class="btn btn-outline-secondary">
            Next: Docker vs Kubernetes for Microservices &rarr;
        </a>
    </div>
</div>

<!-- Copy Script -->
<script>
    document.querySelectorAll('.copy-btn').forEach(btn => {
        btn.addEventListener('click', function () {
            const codeText = this.parentElement.querySelector('code')?.innerText;
            if (codeText) {
                navigator.clipboard.writeText(codeText).then(() => {
                    alert('Code copied to clipboard!');
                });
            }
        });
    });
</script>
